WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:05.860
In the last few years, a lot more machine learning teams have been talking about comparing

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.440
the machine learning systems to human level performance.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.440
Why is this?

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.360
I think there are two main reasons.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.580
First is that because of advances in deep learning, machine learning algorithms are suddenly

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:21.260
working much better and so it's become much more feasible in a lot of application areas

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.020
of machine learning algorithms to actually become competitive with human level performance.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.380
Second, it turns out that the workflow of designing and building a machine learning system,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.540
the workflow is much more efficient when you're trying to do something that humans can also do.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.540
So in those settings, it becomes natural to talk about comparing or trying to mimic human

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.540
level performance.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.100
Let's see a couple of examples of what this means.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.500
I've seen on a lot of machine learning tasks that as you work on a problem over time,

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:57.340
so the x-axis time, this could be many months or even many years over which some team or

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.180
some research community is working on a problem.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.540
Progress tends to be relatively rapid as you approach human level performance.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.800
But then after a while, the algorithm surpasses human level performance and then progress

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.020
and accuracy actually slows down and maybe keeps getting better.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.140
But after surpassing human level performance, it can still get better.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:26.420
But performance, the slope of how rapidly accuracy is going up, often that slows down.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:32.860
And the hope is it achieves some theoretical optimum level of performance.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.340
And over time, as you keep creating the algorithm, maybe bigger and bigger models on more and

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:44.300
more data, the performance approaches but never surpasses some theoretical limits, which

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:53.870
is called the Bayes optimal error.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:02:01.460
So Bayes optimal error, think of this as the best possible error in that there's just no

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:09.460
way for any function mapping from x to y to surpass this person level of accuracy.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:15.140
So for example, for speech recognition, if x is audio clips, some audio is just so noisy

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.420
it's impossible to tell what is in the correct transcription.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.780
So the perfect error may not be 100%.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:29.820
Or for cat recognition, maybe some images are so blurry that it's just impossible for

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:34.740
anyone or anything to tell whether or not there's a cat in that picture.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:40.220
So the perfect level of accuracy may not be 100%.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:46.420
And Bayes optimal error, or Bayesian optimal error, or sometimes Bayes error for short,

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:56.250
is the very best theoretical function for mapping from x to y that can never be surpassed.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:00.530
So it should be no surprise that this purple line, no matter how many years you work on

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.330
the problem, you can never surpass Bayes error or Bayes optimal error.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:11.330
And it turns out that progress is often quite fast until you surpass human level performance.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:16.010
And it sometimes slows down after you surpass human level performance.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:20.810
And I think there are two reasons for that, for why progress often slows down when you surpass

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.250
human level performance.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:28.290
One reason is that human level performance is for many tasks not that far from Bayes optimal error.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.970
You know, people are very good at looking at images and telling if there's a cat or

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.610
listening to audio and transcribing it.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.730
So by the time you surpass human level performance, maybe there's not that much

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.290
headroom to still improve.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.450
But the second reason is that so long as your performance is worse than human level

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.490
performance, then there are actually certain tools you could use to improve performance

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.770
that are harder to use once you surpass human level performance.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.070
So here's what I mean.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:04.870
For tasks that humans are quite good at, and this includes looking at pictures and

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:10.110
recognizing things, listening to audio or reading language, really natural data tasks

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.790
humans tend to be very good at.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:16.230
For tasks that humans are good at, so long as your machine learning algorithm is still

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:20.470
worse than a human, you can get labeled data from humans.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:26.070
That is, you can ask people, ask higher humans to label examples for you so that

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:29.390
you can have more data to feed to your learning algorithm.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.870
Something we'll talk about next week is manual error analysis.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.710
But so long as humans are still performing better than your learning

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.830
algorithm, you can ask people to look at examples that your algorithm is getting

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.470
wrong and try to gain insight in terms of why a person got it right, but the

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:44.350
algorithm got wrong.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:48.070
And we'll see next week that this helps improve your performance.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:51.670
And you can also get a better analysis of bias and variance, which

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:53.110
we'll talk about a little bit.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:57.390
But so long as your algorithm is still doing worse than humans, you have these

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.150
important tactics for improving your algorithm.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.470
Whereas once your algorithm is doing better than humans, then these three

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.110
tactics are harder to apply.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:11.350
So this is maybe another reason why comparing to human level performance is

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:19.430
helpful, especially on tasks that humans do well and why machine learning

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.990
algorithms tend to be really good at trying to replicate tasks that people can

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.470
do and trying to catch up to and maybe slightly surpass human level

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:29.230
performance.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.190
In particular, even though you know what is bias and what is variance, it

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:37.390
turns out that knowing how well humans can do well on a task can help

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:41.350
you understand better how much you should try to reduce bias and how you

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.430
should try to reduce variance.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.750
I want to show you an example of this in the next video.
